ion: relative;"><div class=3D"markdown-content_markdown__cVmKc"><p>The proo=
f of why the solution works is important to understand. Following is its su=
mmary:</p>
<ul>
<li>Consider we start with i =3D 0 and j =3D height.size() - 1. That is, i =
points to the first column and j points to the last column.</li>
<li>Now suppose that <code>h(i)&gt;h(j)</code> (we are not loosing generali=
ty by this assumption)</li>
<li>We calculate the water capacity for the i, j. It will be <code>h(j)*(j-=
i)</code>.</li>
<li>Now see that if we fix j at height.size() - 1 and vary i, we cannot get=
 a greater water capacity. Why?
<ul>
<li>capacity =3D min of both heights * width between them. Since capacity i=
s the product of these two terms, we will look at each term individually.</=
li>
<li>First about the width. It is easy to see that for all other i's (<code>=
i =3D 1, 2,... ,height.size()-2</code>) we will have a lesser width.</li>
<li>Second, the height will be the minimum of the column at i and at j, i.e=
. <code>min(h(i),h(j))</code>. But this value will be always less than <cod=
e>h(j)</code></li>
<li>So both factors in the calculation of the capacity will be smaller and =
hence we can skip considering all the cases where <code>i =3D 1, 2, 3, ...,=
 height.size()-2 and j =3D height.size()-1</code></li>
<li>Which basically means that we can simply move j to j-1.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This is how I understood it and I hope this explanation makes it easy to=
 understand.</p></div><span style=3D"font-size: 0px; line-height: 0;">&nbsp=
